<section class="outcomes"><div class="shell"><div class="orow"><div class="o"><b>~33%</b><span>increase in conversion</span></div><div class="o"><b>+21%</b><span>increase in session time</span></div><div class="o"><b>~47%</b><span>increase in GMV from the app</span></div></div></div></section><section class="ch split slim" id="problem"><div class="shell">
 <div class="ch-txt"><p class="lab">The problem</p><h2 class="claim">Most orders still came in offline, through sales agents.</h2>
 <p>Tradeling sells across 16 categories. Grocery and electronics bring in the most revenue. But the app showed every business everything. A grocer who wanted to buy water and oil saw power tools on the same screen.</p>
 <p>One in five sessions ended in under ten seconds. Most orders still came through sales agents, who placed them for the customer.</p></div>
</div></section><section class="ch split r7" id="research"><div class="shell">
 <div class="ch-txt"><p class="lab">Research</p><h2 class="claim">Two quarters of analytics barely moved adoption. So I went to the shops.</h2>
 <p>Google Analytics, UXCam recordings and competitor reviews gave us small fixes, but customers still did not use the app. I pushed for field research: <b>20+ visits</b> to shops, time with sales agents, and watching owners while they placed orders.</p></div>
 <div class="ch-media"><div class="stack2"><figure class="panel " style="--ar:880 / 535"><div class="panel-in"><img src="/work/tradeling/i/research-backoffice-digital.png" alt="BackOffice vs digital GMV as a share of each month, January to September 2025: digital stays between about 20% and 41%, BackOffice between about 59% and 80%." width="1760" height="1070" decoding="async"></div></figure><figure class="panel " style="--ar:880 / 535"><div class="panel-in"><img src="/work/tradeling/i/d9fbc877ae50.jpg" alt=""></div></figure></div></div>
</div></section>
<section class="ch" id="found"><div class="shell">
 <p class="lab ins-lab">What owners told me</p>
 <ol class="ins">
  <li><span class="iq">“Only show me what I sell.”</span><p>Every owner said this, big or small. A grocer who has to scroll past power tools does not keep looking. They leave.</p></li>
  <li><span class="iq">“The agent gets me a better price.”</span><p>Buyers thought the fixed price in the app was the worse deal, so they kept ordering through agents.</p></li>
  <li><span class="iq">“Size decides how I buy.”</span><p>Small shops have no storage space, so they order only what they need. Medium businesses buy in bulk and are happy to wait one or two days for a better price.</p></li>
 </ol>
 <aside class="beyond"><p class="lab">Beyond the app</p><p>Restaurants and salons said they would buy products close to their expiry date, or in damaged boxes, if the price was lower. I shared this with the logistics and warehouse teams, so they could sell that stock instead of writing it off.</p></aside>
</div></section><section class="bigq"><div class="shell"><blockquote>How do you design one app for a corner grocer, an electronics shop and a salon, when each one only wants to see what it sells?</blockquote></div></section><section class="ch idea" id="idea"><div class="shell">
 <div class="ch-txt"><p class="lab">The idea</p><h2 class="claim">One app that changes itself for each business that signs in.</h2>
 <p>When a customer signs up, their trade licence shows what type of business they are. That decides their vertical. Grocery and IR (Independent Retailers: the electronics and mobile phone shops) have their own home page, categories and brands. Everyone else sees Default, with the full catalogue. It is still one app with one codebase.</p></div>
